From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 16:55:34 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(bedrock): join offline LAN worlds

Probe NetherNet on the local network and show compatible worlds in a separate account-free screen. Parse the retail 1.26.51 NetherNet layout 7 advertisement so authentication flags and the nonce come from the host instead of LAN assumptions.

For self-signed worlds, create one identity before the NetherNet offer and pass the same token to ViaBedrock login. Keep the responder, network ID, nonce, and login mode on the connection.

Receive complete NetherNet signaling datagrams and repair iOS LAN answers that omit trailing SDP attributes from their declared length. Otherwise the client can time out before the ICE connection opens even though discovery succeeded.

Place the LAN subtitle and world list immediately beneath the VFP logo. This screen has no search bar or tabs, so the extra header space obscured the list.

Use core AuthData.createSelfSigned for LAN and account-free HTTP offers.
Transfer the exact pre-handshake token and key independently of LAN
metadata, and preserve them for game login. Exact offline BDS1.26.51.1
rejects unsigned HTTP offers but opens the signed self identity transport.
Online account tokens retain their existing path.

Label the server browser button Bedrock LAN Worlds so users can distinguish native discovery from the Java LAN list.

Use NetherNet for every Bedrock connection. Ordinary host addresses and explicit nethernet:// addresses select HTTP signaling. Preserve upstream's NetherNet-only pipeline and use HTTP capability responses for server status. Request them through ViaBedrock's requestJoinInfo, which tries HTTPS before plaintext like a join, because public servers such as The Hive and CubeCraft answer /v1/join only over TLS. Status pings and joins share upstream's version choice in BedrockConnectionUtil.connectionVersion: the server entry's forced version, or the global target when the entry has none or came from Direct Connect. Joins previously used only the forced version for saved entries, so an entry on the global Bedrock target kept the Java default port 25565 and its signaling request failed, while its ping used 19132. Do not restore legacy transports for fixtures or LAN discovery.

Preserve the unchanged upstream RakNet status classes as source compatibility copies. Compile them without bundling the transport or connecting them to StackAnvil status queries.

